<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mendip Hills</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mendips</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mendip Hills are a range of limestone hills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>. Their lead and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> deposits were mined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> within a few years of the conquest of 43 CE, with lead pigs stamped as early as 49 CE. Mining continued into the 20th century. The hills also contain Cheddar Gorge and Wookey Hole caves.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mendip-hills.jpg" alt="Mendip Hills" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Evans · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crook_Peak_towards_Compton_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The hills run from Weston-super-Mare and the Bristol Channel in the west to the Frome valley in the east, overlooking the Somerset Levels to the south and the Chew Valley to the north. The highest point is Beacon Batch, the summit of Black Down, at 325 metres. They are formed largely of Carboniferous Limestone laid down about 320–350 million years ago and folded in the Variscan orogeny. Erosion and the dissolving of the limestone have left gorges such as Burrington Combe, swallets, limestone pavement and the largest underground river system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Twenty Palaeolithic sites have been identified, eleven of them in caves, and the caves of Cheddar Gorge have yielded many remains, among them the Cheddar Man, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s oldest complete skeleton, found in Gough's Cave. The more than 200 scheduled ancient monuments include the Priddy Nine Barrows and Ashen Hill barrow cemeteries and the forts at Dolebury Warren. Mining dates back to the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and increased after the Roman invasion, and a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fosse-way">Fosse Way</a> served the lead mines at Charterhouse.</p><p>In 1789 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a> noted the hard conditions of the area, which led Hannah More to begin her work for the Mendip miners and agricultural workers. In the 18th and early 19th centuries 7,300 hectares of common heathland on the hills were enclosed. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> a bombing decoy on Black Down was built to confuse bombers aiming at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>, and in the 1960s the 293-metre Mendip television mast went up on Pen Hill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a>. According to legend, Augustus Montague Toplady wrote the words of Rock of Ages while sheltering under a rock in Burrington Combe in 1763.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The higher western part, covering 198 square kilometres, has been an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/area-of-outstanding-natural-beauty">Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty</a> since 1972. Nine active quarries supply road stone to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The hills are a national centre for caving and cave diving: after an attempt at Swildon's Hole in 1934, the first successful British cave dive was made at Wookey Hole Caves, whose sump is the deepest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mendip Hills” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The province exported grain, wool, hunting dogs, lead and silver from the <b>Mendips</b> and Derbyshire, and tin from Cornwall; the British woollen cloak, the birrus Britannicus, appears in Diocletian's price edict of 301.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
